WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:05.110
This video is about evolution.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:10.570
Not the biological evolution you’re familiar
with, where living organisms adapt to their

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:11.570
environment.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.600
No, this is about virtual evolution.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.860
About discovering the extent to which video
games and other simulations can shatter the

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:23.340
natural order and let us experiment with the
building blocks of life itself.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:24.340
…And the extent to which they can make us
laugh.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.590
The ambitious dream of using computers to
simulate life is an old one, and you might

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.230
be amazed at just how far the technology has
come.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:37.291
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
take natural selection into our own hands,

00:00:37.291 --> 00:01:02.340
and see how far we can push this concept before
things get out of control...

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:09.720
Our story begins in 1970, when British mathematician
John H. Conway unveiled a game simply called

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.170
‘Life.’

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:16.869
The rules are simple: within a blank void,
you can create tiny pixels called cells, which

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:21.800
will reproduce if there are enough cells next
to them, but die out if they get overcrowded

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.180
or end up alone.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.420
Simple, right?

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:33.799
Well, you activate the simulation, and then…

00:01:33.799 --> 00:01:38.960
Using these rules and basic starting patterns,
Conway’s Game of Life can create unbelievably

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.430
complex chain reactions that legitimately
feel…

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.150
well, alive.

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:49.020
Pretty quickly, people discovered that depending
on your initial pattern, specific “species”

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.210
would emerge with their own unique behavior.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.619
Microbe-like colonies of pixels that appeared
to move were nicknamed “Spaceships.”

00:01:56.619 --> 00:02:00.719
So called “Puffer Trains” traveled along
and left behind a trail of debris.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:04.750
“Rakes” left behind trails of fully functional
Spaceships.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:10.030
This pattern called the Rabbits starts small,
but expands for a ridiculous amount of in-game

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.819
time before stabilizing.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:15.450
It’s a lot like watching cells under a microscope.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:20.180
Of course, the pixels in the game of life
aren’t actually ‘alive’ by most definitions,

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:25.140
but they’re an incredible starting point
that show just how effective simple rules

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.849
can be at creating complex emergent behaviors.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:32.200
It’s proven to be a game with almost limitless
potential.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.860
But we can take things further.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:41.390
Life Engine is a more recent game by the amazing
youtuber Emergent Garden that simulates not

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:44.180
just emergent behavior, but natural selection.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:49.930
Like Conway’s game, Life Engine is all about
pixels, but here, different colored pixels

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:51.330
have different functions.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:56.140
Orange pixels act as a mouth, allowing lifeforms
to consume energy.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.890
Green pixels produce energy like plants.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.400
Blue pixels can move about like animals.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:07.489
Pink pixels do damage to other lifeforms,
and purple pixels act like armor against damage.

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:13.190
Finally, grey pixels act as eyes, allowing
the smartest virtual lifeforms to avoid each

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.530
other.

00:03:14.530 --> 00:03:19.500
When you begin a game in Life Engine, it always
starts with a mass of stationary lifeforms

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.989
slowly expanding out from the center via reproduction.

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:28.330
Yet each time a pixel reproduces, it has a
random chance of changing color, simulating

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.030
a genetic mutation.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:35.970
As with real world natural selection, some
of the mutations, like movement, prove advantageous,

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.930
and give that specific lifeform more opportunities
to reproduce.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:45.250
In almost every game, fast moving lifeforms
quickly overwhelm the stationary plant-like

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.150
creatures, and then settle into an equilibrium
much like on Earth.

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:54.590
From here, this simulated petri dish becomes
a battleground, with different species evolving

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:58.920
weapons, armor, and evasive maneuvers to out
compete each other.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:05.629
A similar youtuber-created game is The Bibites,
a simulator where endearing virtual creatures

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:07.209
compete for food.

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:12.450
This game also works on a system of random
mutation and natural selection, with the titular

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.720
Bibites getting more and more complicated
the more you play.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:20.940
There’s more both games than this, and there’s
a link to them, and all the games and experiments

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:24.620
discussed in this video, in the description.

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:30.139
There are a lot of incredible games in this
category of cell-like evolution simulators.

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:35.340
Like the highly underrated Cute Mold by Erytau,
which lets you simulate the growth of different

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.360
slime molds.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:42.400
Or the remarkable Clusters by algorithmic
artist Jeffery Ventrella, that renders a microworld

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.700
of particle systems that begin to display
increasingly complex behaviors not due to

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:53.540
some hidden AI, but purely as the result of
a system based on the laws of physics.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:58.110
Many of these games are inspired by Conway’s
Life in some capacity, but all have their

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.729
own unique spin.

00:04:59.729 --> 00:05:04.360
There’s one final computational model that
demands attention.

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:10.240
Developed by scholar Bert Chan in 2015, Lenia
is an award-winning program derived from Conway's

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:16.560
Game of Life that uses continuous generalization
to make everything smooth and ethereal.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:22.080
Using high resolution patterns, Lenia produces
far more complicated types of digital life,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.919
with over four hundred individual species
discovered.

00:05:25.919 --> 00:05:30.300
Lenia patterns have displayed an impressive
number of the traits we typically associate

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:36.360
with biological life, including bilaterally
and radially symmetrical body plans, reproduction,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.080
and even intercommunication between colonies.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:45.130
Chan has even organized these patterns into
a taxonomic tree of evolution, some of which

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:48.789
are so advanced some have called them ‘Mathematical
Lifeforms.’

00:05:48.789 --> 00:05:53.990
And sure, by most definitions these patterns
aren’t truly ‘alive’ — they’re just

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:56.979
the result of rules interacting in unique
ways.

00:05:56.979 --> 00:06:02.699
But in a way, our universe and all the incredible
systems of life within are also the result

00:06:02.699 --> 00:06:04.960
of rules interacting in unique ways.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.330
And these simulations are only getting more
advanced…

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:15.199
Following the path of life on Earth, it’s
time to leave the virtual tidepool of cell

00:06:15.199 --> 00:06:20.210
simulators, and take our first, very awkward
steps onto dry land.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.280
Evolution is a game by programmer Keiwan that
allows you to build digital animals by connecting

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.530
joints with bones, and bones with AI controlled
muscle tendons.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:33.740
The game’s neural network will then try
its absolute best to get the creature to walk

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:38.699
forwards over multiple generations, with each
attempt appearing as a little ‘ghost’

00:06:38.699 --> 00:06:40.060
next to the current model.

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:46.280
At first, most simulations are an absolute
mess of failed attempts, but with enough generations,

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:50.610
the neural network will eventually get better
at moving, even if it’s not in the way you

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:51.610
expect.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:56.100
And that’s because this AI doesn’t know
what ‘walking’ looks like, which is why,

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.330
at first, it’s quite bad at it.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:02.430
Every movement it learns is through trial
and error — essentially running off its

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.509
own version of natural selection.

00:07:04.509 --> 00:07:09.460
And with enough practice, these simulated
creatures can evolve movement patterns that

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:11.760
are reasonably close to the real thing.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:15.720
And it’s not just walking that this network
is capable of.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:19.760
Depending on the design of your creature,
it might learn to roll, or hop around like

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:23.139
a kangaroo, or even take to the skies.

00:07:23.139 --> 00:07:27.300
And there are more challenging tasks available
than walking in a straight line.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:32.509
You can try to make the poor creature climb
an infinite staircase, or jump over fast moving

00:07:32.509 --> 00:07:33.770
obstacles.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:38.490
While at times hilarious, there is a strange
tragedy in watching these innocent creatures

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.650
struggle against gravity.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:45.240
Something about this game really captures
the unfeeling nature of natural selection,

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.040
where advancement often involves countless
generations of failure.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:55.690
Though cleverly simplified, this is, in many
ways, the story of life on Earth.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:01.461
A similar game to Evolution is Evol Pedal
— a walking simulator created by Evol Games

00:08:01.461 --> 00:08:06.400
where a lifeform made up of randomly mutating
blocks learns to travel forwards.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:11.610
The game also has an amazing ‘3D version’
where a CGI model of a human will learn to

00:08:11.610 --> 00:08:17.300
walk by any means necessary, which can have
both amusing and unsettling results.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:22.400
With over 100 moving parts, it’s difficult
for the AI to stay coordinated.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.120
You can also adjust the settings to allow
for non-human movements like knees that bend

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.289
backwards, which often leads to even more
uncanny sights.

00:08:31.289 --> 00:08:36.000
The game even lets you fully adjust the gravity,
and pushing the setting too far can lead to

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.029
things breaking down a bit.

00:08:38.029 --> 00:08:44.110
Such is the price of glorious, glorious evolution…

00:08:44.110 --> 00:08:47.279
But maybe we’re taking too many steps too
quickly.

00:08:47.279 --> 00:08:52.130
After all, life on our planet didn’t first
evolve to move on land, but in the much more

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.590
forgiving environment of water.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:09:00.550
In 2016, a group of scientists used artificial
evolution to successfully produce swimming,

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:02.580
soft-bodied creatures.

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.529
These simulated lifeforms were made of squares
of virtual tissue, with green representing

00:09:07.529 --> 00:09:11.589
actively moving tissue, and blue resenting
passive tissues.

00:09:11.589 --> 00:09:17.280
The AI found that using a combination of both
resulted in the most efficient swimming methods,

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:21.670
producing a range of body types convergent
with various forms of life in our own oceans.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:26.529
And to study the relationship between swimming
and walking, some of these creatures were

00:09:26.529 --> 00:09:31.920
moved onto land, where they found clever ways
to use bodies evolved for aquatic motion to

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:33.790
navigate a terrestrial setting.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:34.790
These experiments essentially created virtual
intertidal life, offering intriguing insight

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:35.790
into the evolution of early land animals.

00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:40.560
That same year, another group of scientists
ran a similar soft-bodied creature experiment

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.390
where a network evolved a body from a single
initial cell.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:50.210
Each evolutionary run produced something different,
but many looked remarkably close to biological

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:51.210
life.

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:55.770
It's worth remembering that this AI didn’t
know what a leg or a tail is — it just found

00:09:55.770 --> 00:10:00.470
a way to keep balanced that is convergent
with life on Earth.

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:05.130
On the subject of muscle-based locomotion,
one of the most well-known experiments comes

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:10.690
from 2013, with a 3D simulation of various
bipedal creatures.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:15.330
These models learned to walk through trial
and error over multiple generations.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:20.529
No motion capture or keyframe animation was
used, with the impressive final results the

00:10:20.529 --> 00:10:22.400
product of AI learning.

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:27.029
The examiners then pitted this AI against
different target speeds, which led to the

00:10:27.029 --> 00:10:29.410
creatures adjusting their gait accordingly.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:34.830
They even put them through obstacle courses,
throwing virtual boxes at them and adding

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:37.089
random slopes to their environments.

00:10:37.089 --> 00:10:42.279
While sometimes they’d succeed in catching
the AI off guard, the overall results were

00:10:42.279 --> 00:10:45.270
strikingly consistent.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:50.670
A more recent experiment presented at the
ICLR conference used an analogous method to

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.110
teach different models to walk.

00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:58.320
Many of these results resemble a three-dimensional
version of one of the evolution video games,

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:01.410
as both are attempting to simulate similar
things.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:06.820
But these scientific simulations are not games
as we understand them, although this technology

00:11:06.820 --> 00:11:13.870
may one day be used to make virtual entertainment
more lifelike.

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:19.149
As amazing as all these simulations and video
games are, for me, there’s always been that

00:11:19.149 --> 00:11:24.709
faint dream of the one unified game, that
combines cellular models with aquatic and

00:11:24.709 --> 00:11:29.820
terrestrial locomotion to create the ultimate
evolution-simulating masterpiece.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:36.310
For a lot of people that dream once had a
name — and its name was ‘Spore.’

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:42.370
Released in the late 2000s, the buildup to
Spore was, to put it mildly, completely nuts.

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:48.050
As early as 2005 demos of this hyper-detailed
procedurally generated universe simulator

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:51.050
were being shown, and it looked incredible.

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:55.840
There was a sense among some before its release
that this game, which seemed to merge science

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.760
with infinite customization, would change
the industry forever.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.270
And then the game came out.

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:07.820
While Spore did impress with its creativity
and ambition, a lot of people felt the game

00:12:07.820 --> 00:12:13.760
was significantly shallower and less, uh,
scientific than they were led to believe.

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:18.830
To be clear, I’m still quite a fan of this
game despite its eccentricities — just looking

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:21.550
at it gives me a huge hit of nostalgia.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:26.870
And it does explore a number of the principles
of evolution and biology in its own unique

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:27.870
manner, which I’ve made two separate videos
about.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:33.560
But it certainly takes a more cartoonish approach
to the stages of life, and some features advertised

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:37.740
like an underwater multicellular stage weren’t
in the final game.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:43.010
For many, it wasn’t the comprehensive evolution
simulator they were hoping for…

00:12:43.010 --> 00:12:47.010
Yet the dream of Spore hasn’t vanished just
yet.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:51.310
Recently, there has been a surge of new games
that seem to take some of the concepts of

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:53.610
Spore and push them further.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:58.510
One game on this exciting new frontier is
Adapt by Paul Hervé.

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:02.779
Currently a work in progress, Adapt is a game
that lets you build your own lifeform much

00:13:02.779 --> 00:13:08.230
like Spore’s creature stage, although it
has a greater emphasis on biological plausibility

00:13:08.230 --> 00:13:11.860
and finding your niche through careful evolutionary
choices.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:17.740
And unlike Spore, Adapt lets you build a creature
specially adapted for a semi-aquatic lifestyle,

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:18.740
which is a neat addition.

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:23.680
The game is currently free to play on Steam,
and is a promising addition to this emerging

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:26.529
subgenre.

00:13:26.529 --> 00:13:32.920
One evolution simulator that really impresses
with its sheer amount of detail is The Sapling.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:38.330
Created by Wessel Stoop, this polygon-art
sandbox game lets you design every organism

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:43.279
in an ecosystem, and then simulate their interactions
over multiple generations.

00:13:43.279 --> 00:13:49.320
And I do mean every organism — unlike most
games in this genre, you’re not just designing

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:53.010
animals, but plants too, and even things like
algae.

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:57.360
And the depth of these systems are absolutely
mind-blowing.

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:02.019
When placing a plant, you need to consider
how many leaves it has relative to the sunlight,

00:14:02.019 --> 00:14:06.829
and the length of its roots, and the amount
of ground water in the area, and the soil

00:14:06.829 --> 00:14:10.450
softness, and the temperature, and the wind
strength.

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:14.279
Different plant colors realistically grow
better or worse depending on the type of star

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:15.490
your planet orbits.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:19.570
There’s a full system of flowers you can
add to plants that produce different amounts

00:14:19.570 --> 00:14:22.510
of pollen that affects reproductivity.

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:27.350
You can also use bioluminescence in plants
to attract pollinators — or use bioluminescence

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:29.759
in animals to mislead prey.

00:14:29.759 --> 00:14:34.959
In terms of creatures, there are flying lifeforms,
detailed customization options for aquatic

00:14:34.959 --> 00:14:40.490
life, different eyes that can see different
ranges of infrared and ultraviolet light.

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:45.230
You can even adjust the instincts of each
animal, using a modular system of commands

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:50.350
to change how they react to different creatures,
sounds frequencies, and specific colors.

00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:52.720
It’s absolutely off the chain.

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:58.269
Especially when you consider that like Adapt,
The Sapling was developed by just one person.

00:14:58.269 --> 00:15:03.010
Compared to Spore which was made by a goliath
of a team, these single-developer games of

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:07.600
course don’t have the same resources behind
them, but the incredible results show that

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:12.279
there are still passionate people who believe
in this idea — this dream of a game that

00:15:12.279 --> 00:15:14.810
could simulate life like never before.

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:21.120
And if we’re going to talk about dreamers,
we have to talk about Thrive.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:26.250
Beginning development over a decade ago, Thrive
is a game quite unambiguously born out of

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.120
frustration with the final product of Spore.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:34.230
A communal project open to all who wish to
contribute, the game takes a far more ambitious

00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:37.750
and scientific approach to Spore’s gameplay
loop.

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:42.209
In the game, you start as a single, realistic
cell in a tidepool.

00:15:42.209 --> 00:15:48.480
As you advance, your cell splits into a complex,
multicellular organism, becoming a 3D model

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:53.709
that can be fully shaped by the player, with
different organs serving unique functions.

00:15:53.709 --> 00:15:58.649
Over the next few stages, this creature grows
in size and intelligence, eventually able

00:15:58.649 --> 00:16:03.510
to communicate using a multifaceted language
system and create technology.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:07.959
Until eventually the technology compounds
to the point where you ascend to a god-like

00:16:07.959 --> 00:16:11.360
level of power and can control the entire
galaxy.

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:13.889
It’s all so cool.

00:16:13.889 --> 00:16:17.370
It also doesn’t exist.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:21.690
Since Thrive a crowd-sourced game, anyone
on the internet can come up with ideas for

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:26.560
Thrive, but actually implementing those ideas
is another story.

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:32.170
Most of the images people share of the game’s
later stages is actually art by the talented

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:33.170
Sciocont.

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:37.911
Right now, over a decade after the game’s
inception, the only playable stage is the

00:16:37.911 --> 00:16:40.040
first one: The Microbe Stage.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:43.750
And to be clear, this initial stage is very
interesting.

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:49.440
It’s an exceedingly complex and realistic
cell simulator, where instead of eating cartoon

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:54.269
plants, you’re balancing your Glucose, Ammonia,
and Phosphate levels, in many ways making

00:16:54.269 --> 00:16:56.750
it a game about virtual chemistry.

00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:00.699
While this attention to scientific detail
makes the game fairly difficult, it’s a

00:17:00.699 --> 00:17:02.990
truly admirable product.

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:07.530
Another cool thing about Thrive is that while
you build and design your cell, other cells

00:17:07.530 --> 00:17:13.540
in the environment randomly mutate and adapt,
with any given game creating hundreds of interconnected

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:19.289
microbe lineages, all with their own randomly
generated scientific name.

00:17:19.289 --> 00:17:24.120
Most people seem to be aware that the other
stages of Thrive aren’t coming for a while.

00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:29.110
But it is pretty inspiring to see so many
people drawn in by this idea, and shows that

00:17:29.110 --> 00:17:36.490
the dream of the ultimate evolution simulator
hasn’t gone extinct just yet.

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:41.070
Before this journey through virtual evolution
concludes, there’s one last thing I have

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:42.230
to show you.

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:47.690
A simulation that does something incredible
not by thinking big, but by thinking small.

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:49.250
Really small.

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:54.289
OpenWorm is an international open science
project with the sole purpose of simulating

00:17:54.289 --> 00:17:59.000
every cell of a type of worm that grows just
under 1mm in length.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:04.110
That includes every neuron in its body, the
wiring that connects these neurons, muscle

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:07.090
cells, organs, and even its genetic code.

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:11.870
The current model is so complete that when
placed into an environmental simulation, it

00:18:11.870 --> 00:18:13.840
can move like a worm.

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:19.230
As incredible as this recreation is however,
it is still not a perfect match, with replicating

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:22.710
the electrical signals within the brain a
future hurdle.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:27.570
But it may only be a matter of time…

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:31.950
As our ability to simulate life gets more
and more accurate, I can’t help but wonder

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:34.190
about our responsibilities.

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:39.120
Over the course of making this video I caused
thousands of virtual lifeforms to expire for

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:42.049
the purpose of education and entertainment.

00:18:42.049 --> 00:18:46.620
And sure, these ‘creatures’ were pixels,
just ones and zeros.

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:51.309
But looking at how our ability to simulate
evolution has ‘evolved,’ it’s clear

00:18:51.309 --> 00:18:53.800
that things are advancing rapidly.

00:18:53.800 --> 00:19:00.159
If one day we are able to truly render a universe
in a box, filled with virtual creatures indistinguishable

00:19:00.159 --> 00:19:05.320
from biological life, I wonder if we’ll
have to start having more serious conversations

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:07.650
about what we do with that power?

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:10.950
We know how people play The Sims, after all…

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:15.270
Perhaps one day, we’ll think of those first
pixels placed in Conway’s Game of Life as

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:20.130
comparable to the first cells dividing in
Earth’s primordial oceans — the opening

00:19:20.130 --> 00:19:24.750
of a Pandora’s Box that marks a new chapter
in the story of life.

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:29.760
And what that chapter looks like will be up
to us…

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:34.810
Existential musings aside, the future of this
subgenre looks quite promising.

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:38.910
There are plenty of other games that feature
evolution in some way that I didn’t get

00:19:38.910 --> 00:19:40.570
a chance to talk about.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:44.669
If you found this subject interesting, once
again, there are links to all the games and

00:19:44.669 --> 00:19:49.520
scientific papers discussed in this video
down below, with more information about the

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:54.860
incredible people who are paving the way in
simulating evolution.

00:19:54.860 --> 00:19:57.250
As always, thanks for watching.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:01.680
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:05.380
the notification icon to stay up to date on
